bskendig@netcom.com (Brian Kendig) wrote:
>I desperately need to find an EISA configuration utility (ECU) disk for
>a DEC Alpha AXP 150 computer.

The internal code name of the AXP 150 was "Jensen".  Tell whoever you're 
talking to at Digital to find the order number for the ECU disk via the 
JENSEN internal notes conference.  If ordering one isn't fast enough for 
you, perhaps you can build one yourself: the ECU disk actually consists 
of two parts: an executable (mumble.exe) and a bunch of configuration 
files (mumble.cfg).  If you have the executable already (e.g. from 
an OS distribution kit) you may be able to get the .cfg files via ftp.

The .cfg files are specific to both the operating system (NT or Unix/VMS)
and the device being configured, but the executable is not.  Some OS
updates have included ECU diskettes.  I think my DEC OSF/1 V3.2B kit had
one. Your NT kit may also have one!

From a licensing point of view, I believe only the ECU executable is
a separately-licensed product.  If you're not sure whether you have a
valid license, conscience demands that you order a copy.  But I've been
told that you are free to copy the .cfg files.  

>Just how
>out-of-date *is* an AXP 150, anyway?

A Jensen is not *that* old.  I have one and run VMS and NT on it.  But
I must admit my AlphaStation 400 4/233 feels rather more responsive.

Hope this helps
