newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <49d6o7$3fh@holmes.ee.ubc.ca>, Shyan Ku <shyank> writes:

>Hello.
>
>If two hard disks on a DEC3000 have been partitioned as one logical disk,
>is it possible to repartition them into two separate disks without reformatting
>them?

Yes.  But probably not without losing the data.  It depends on what
was used to make the single logical volume.  If LVM was used, then
you need to destroy the volume, and either create two new volumes
or remove all traces that LVM was ever used and just go back to the
two disks.  Either way, the data will be lost in the process.

If you're using LSM, then there are a number of different possibilities.
If the two disks are in a mirror, then just remove one of the disks
from the mirror and use it for a different volume.  The remaining
disk will still have the data.  If the disk were a striped volume or
concatenated, then the answer is the same as for LVM.

If you're using Advfs, then I believe removal of one of the disks
will start the migration of the data to the other disk.  All the
data had better fit.

If you're using something else, it would be useful to know what.

I should note that for me, "reformatting" means running the protocol
specific low level formatter on/of the disk.  Ordinarily there is no
reason to reformat.

