From: henning@i4get.enet.dec.com (John L. Henning)
Subject: re: dual dec processors
Date: 1995/11/29
Message-ID: <49hitq$3h1@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 120400634
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


swensen@aros.net (Dave Swensen) wrote:
>Does anyone have any experience running with a dec 2000 or 2100 dual processor 
>machine? 
>What I am wondering is whether I will see true 2x speed if I run 2 jobs 
>simultaneously (one job on each processor). Or does memory access become a 
>bottleneck in these machines (especially when accessing that much data) ?

It depends not so much on how MUCH data you have, as on the 
access pattern.  

A key recipe for getting good performance out of RISC systems 
with fast CPUs but only moderately-fast memory systems is to 
structure your code & data to make the best use of the caches 
that you can, so that when a cache fill occurs from main memory 
you don't end up wasting bandwidth.  Kevin Dowd's _High 
Performance Computing_ (O'Reilly & Associates) has very good 
material on this subject. 

If you are using your caches well, and have plenty of computation
to do, then compilers can "hide" the memory access time underneath
the computations.  But if you have a problem which for one reason
or another hops through memory like a random number generator on 
a pogo stick, and does minimal computation, then your speed will 
be limited by your memory system.  In that situation, the McCalpin 
Streams benchmark can be useful, as it does almost nothing BUT 
access main memory.

On the 2100 @ 190 Mhz, we quoted 80 MB/sec for 1 CPU and 130 MB/sec 
for 2 CPUs.  So if you have reason to believe that your access 
pattern is one that is limited strictly by main memory speed, you 
would not expect a 2x speedup with 2 processors on a 2100 - more 
like 1.6.  On the other hand, with an AlphaServer 8400 5/300, 1 
CPU is good for 190 MB/sec and the speedup is 1.81 with 2 CPUs, 
3.28 with 4, 4.28 with 6.

Qualifier: the above statistics are for "Programmer-perceived"
bandwidth as measured by one specific benchmark, namely McCalpin
Streams.  McCalpin bandwidth is often about 1/2 of vendor-quoted
"raw" bandwidth or peak bandwidth or theoretical bandwidth, although 
I know of one case (not Alpha) where the McCalpin bandwidth is 
about 1/8 of the vendor-quoted raw bandwidth.  Do not compare 
McCalpin bandwidth to raw bandwidths.

      /John Henning
       CSD Performance Group
       Digital Equipment Corporation
       henning@zko.dec.com
       Speaking for myself, not Digital



