Subject: Re: I-Cache consistency
Date: 1995/11/29
Message-ID: <49htlj$lrn@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 120495516
references: <49fgva$cfp@news.tuwien.ac.at>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.2N (Windows; I; 16bit)

anton@a0.complang.tuwien.ac.at (Anton Ertl) wrote:
>I am working on an application that writes code into memory and then
>executes it. Therefore I need to make sure that the I-cache does not
>contain stale code. Is there a system call etc. on the Alpha for doing
>this (like cachectl under Ultrix/MIPS) or is it unnecessary? I have
>looked in the documentation and in the header files, but found
>nothing.
>
>I am currently working with OSF/1, but would also appreciate info on
>other OSs.


   Try the CALL_PAL IMB instruction (Instruction Memory Barrier).  This 
is a required PALcode instruction that is intended for exactly the type 
of code that you are writing.  You must execute this instruction before 
executing any code that was written into the instruction stream or the
results will be unpredicatable.  

   See the "Alpha Architecture Reference Manual" (edited by Richard 
Sites, available from Digital Press) for more info.

Brian Hibbert





