From: jfc@mit.edu (John Carr)
Subject: Re: FreeBSD for VMS?
Date: 1995/11/26
Message-ID: <498hda$121@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 120097574
references: <490ed4$gjj@mars.hyperk.com> <DIK78o.Hnv@midway.uchicago.edu> <30b6ab0e.0@xyzzy.cs.colorado.edu>
organization: Massachusetts Institute Of Technology
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

BSD 4.4 picked up parts of Mach's memory management so the VM has to be
rewritten for VAX (but if it's running on a VAX, that has been done).
After that, there is a lot of device-dependent code to write and limited
documentation for the processors, I/O busses, and devices.  For example,
cache control is different on each generation of VAX.  There is no code
outside DEC to drive the SPX display hardware on many post-1989 VAXstations
(you can get a text interface using the ROM routines if you know the entry
points).  Turbochannel systems require special code.  And so on.  The work
adds up.

-- 
    John Carr (jfc@mit.edu)



