In article <498hda$121@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>, jfc@mit.edu (John Carr) writes:
> BSD 4.4 picked up parts of Mach's memory management so the VM has to be
> rewritten for VAX (but if it's running on a VAX, that has been done).
> After that, there is a lot of device-dependent code to write and limited
> documentation for the processors, I/O busses, and devices.  For example,
> cache control is different on each generation of VAX.

Mt.Xinu wrote the cache control and machine-check code for the CVAX
(which is what the orginial poster wanted) for 4.3-Tahoe, and posted
it to the Net with a BSD copyright in the late 80s.  They did VM
code, too; gluing it into a pmap shouldn't be *too* hard, given
the Uvax-II support in NetBSD.

> outside DEC to drive the SPX display hardware on many post-1989 VAXstations
> (you can get a text interface using the ROM routines if you know the entry
> points).  Turbochannel systems require special code.  And so on.

Turbochannel support code for Alphas and Decstations already exists
in NetBSD, as does (less than elegant) support for some of the
DEC-specific ASICs, like the SII SCSI controller used in some
VAXstations. I don't know about the SPX; there are drivers for
the Turbochannel and successor framebuffers that're on-board on Alphas.


> The work adds up.

Of course it does; but  more of the pieces are already extant
than one might think.   (I don't have a vax, or I might try.)



