In article <817058923.12534@leafield.demon.co.uk> writes:
>manson@decus.org.au wrote:
>>In article <bland-071195170818@141.214.12.86>, bland@umich.edu (Peyton Bland) writes:
>>> We have a TLZ06 4mm tape drive on our Alpha.  It has a tape in it that
>>> cannot be removed by the button on the front of the drive.  Things seemed
>if it's external power the unit on/off or if it's internal shutdown
>the system oan power on/off.....

I have an internal, and that didn't do it.  Apparently the tape got
messed up, lost its header, or something.  It would NOT come out.  
Seems it wanted to see a tape there, or be able to read it, before
it would eject it.  I had to take it apart and physically rip it
out--at that point, the tape is down and opened up, and doesn't
lend itself to manual extraction either.

It would appear to be a software bug in the drive, wanting to see
the tape there first.  Maybe it's trying to rewind to the beginning,
or something, but it should fail over and run it out regardless, or at
least have a method to do so, such as holding the button down for an
extended period to signal an eject.

And then I thought it was the drive, so stuck it in a TLZ04--guess
what happened then?  Trashed that tape then, after I got it out.

