Rightfully so... :)  The hardware may be cheap, but DEC tends to up
the cost of licensing old hardware.  Additionally, if you need memory
you _may_ have problems finding it.  It uses a proprietary board, but
a work-alike is available from Dataram.

: Is there anyone out there that has any details on this beast or where
: i could find a FAQ etc

Well, compared to a new VAXstation 4000/90 it is _dog_ slow.  That m38
on the end of the model number really means 3.8 VUPs.  It was the
second generation of the VAXstation 3100 series.  It can address up
to 32MB of memory and has an optional _slow_ SCSI bus.  I believe that
it was based on the NCR 53c90 chip which is a single-ended, SCSI-I
host adapter.  Don't expect outstanding I/O performance.

Basically, we converted all of our 3100-series boxes over to EWS to
use them as X-Tubes and run them against an Alpha 3000/800.  No
overhead for VMS, and 3.8 VUPS makes a nice X Tube.  The only problem
is that the processor has to do all of the graphics primitives - there
isn't a graphics co-processor like on the Alphas with Turbochannel.

