In article <9511212058.AA06824@dxmint.cern.ch>, danpop@hpl3sn03.cern.ch 
says...

>Anyway, third-party application based benchmarks, where nothing is recompiled
>because the source code isn't available,

Just as a matter of interest, does the recent FX!32 announcement change this 
statement at all? Does x86 -> Alpha translating count as recompiling?

> showed exactly the same thing as
>the SPEC benchmarks: a P6-200 is twice as fast as a P5-133.  They also
>show that the NT applications run faster on a P6-166 than on the AlphaStation
>600 5/300 (see http://www.ideas.com.au/bench/bapco/bapco.htm).

Comparing the AlphaStation with a couple of P6s and the top P5:

System                   CPU          CLK SYSmark/NT  SS  PM  WP  Pres CAD
Intergraph TDZ300 GLZ1T  Pentium Pro  200 665         585 833 629 706  602
Intel Alder              Pentium Pro  166 564         504 700 544 592  504
AlphaStation 600 5/300	 21164        300 529         514 491 538 466  653
Dell Dimension XPS P133c Pentium      133 326         303 273 353 384  330

Certainly the overall Sysmark/NT number is better on the Pentium Pros. On the 
other hand, the CAD application comes out better. Here's one NT application 
that runs faster than on a P6-200 (and therefore faster than on a P6-166, and 
nearly twice as fast as on a P5-133).

In the presentation graphics (Pres) benchmark, the Alpha loses heavily, but 
then again, the benchmark involves PowerPoint, so the Alpha is emulating 16 
bit x86 code. I suspect the project management (PM) benchmark involves 
Microsoft Project (more emulated 16 bit x86 code), but I might be wrong there.

As for the spreadsheet (SS) and word processing (WP) benchmarks (Excel and 
Word?), although they're native code, they're still x86 oriented (i.e. not 
RISC friendly code, whatever that means), but let's ignore that. The 5/300 
still beats the P6-166 in the spreadsheet benchmark, and comes pretty close in 
word processing.

So, in this benchmark, some NT applications run faster (and not by much) on a 
P6-166 than an Alpha 5/300. One application (maybe two) is running emulated 
code (but then P6s don't run 16 bit code too well either). One NT application 
runs faster on an Alpha 5/300 than a P6-200.

The benchmark shows that an P6 running 16 bit x86 code is faster than an Alpha 
5/300 emulating 16 bit x86 code. Hardly a fair comparison, but then its the 
application that matters, not what lies underneath, I suppose. On the other 
hand, the Alpha 5/300 emulates 16 bit x86 code faster than a P5-133 runs it 
native.

But then, who believes benchmarks anyway? 8-)
-- 
PJDM
Peter Mayne
Digital Equipment Corporation (Australia)
Canberra, ACT
