>>Now then, to make this reply PDP-11 related: we should open up our
>>PDP-11s and make sure that all of the wiring is properly connected.  A
>>PDP-11 is a terrible thing to have melt.

>Some of the larger power controllers (for example, on my 11/70 in a
>dual-wide hi-boy and I think in most 11/780's) have thermal fuses/breakers
>in them, designed to shut off power if the cabinet becomes too warm.

The 860 and 861 power controllers have little thermostatic switches in 
them (self-reseting) that ground the 'power-off' line if that cabinet 
overheats. There's a similar thermostat in the middle of the BA11-F my 
11/45 CPU is in, designed to shut down the machine if the CPU overheats. 
I've never had any of these trip 'for real', but when I was putting my 
11/45 system together, I made sure each of them would trip when heated 
with a hot-air gun. The installation manual suggests a match flame, but I 
didn't want to set the machine on fire :-)

>And you know that center conductor in the 3-conductor power control cable?
>It's an emergency shutoff bus, designed to shut off all other controllers
>in a system in the case of such an event.  I know it's implemented in
>the larger power controllers, but I don't know about the smaller ones
>you'd typically find on a Q-bus system.

It's certainly implemented in the 871 - see my description of how that 
unit works.

>Tim. (shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu)

