>>- it can't go where it is shown - my guess is that it's put in parallel 

>It can't?  Are my ASCII schematics that difficult to comprehend?  :-(

It's not that, it's the fact that a capacitor in that position is 
electical nonsense - see below.

>>with C2. Anyway, as to how it works.

>C4 is connected to C2 and the cathode of D4, etc. on one side, and to
>Q1, or what turned out to be the 8V regulator, on the other side.

Yes, that's how you've drawn it _but_ look. The top of C2 is the output 
of a DC power supply, and is at approximately constant voltage. We need 
to apply that voltage to the input of 'Q1'. Putting a capacitor C4 in 
there would effectively block said current, and the thing could never 
work - in other words there could be no current into Q1, and hence none 
through the relay.

If there is a component in series with that connection, a resistor would 
be more likely. Are you sure 'C4' is a capacitor ? 


>>When the power off line J2-2 is grounded, then the diode D3 is connected 
>>from the output of the current source to ground. This effectively shorts 
>>out IC1's coil, which thus turns off. In practice, the overheat switch is 
>>connected here to turn off the machine if it overheats.

>Perhaps we should all go add lots of thermistors to our systems,

Actually DEC used a little bimetallic thermostat, I think. There was one in
every 860 and 861 power controller, and even one inside some CPU boxes, 
like my 11/45

