 It sounds like you are doing the right thing, but there may be some
quirks depending on the Mac you're using. 

 What is happening when you try to connect ?

 You also *really* want to re-install fresh MacTCP and MacPPP software if
your making that kind of a switch. It may not say so in the manual, but
you really want to do that.

 Trash:

 System Folder           MacTCP DNR
 Control Panels Folder   MacTCP *
                         ConfigPPP *
 Extensions Folder       PPP *
 Preferences Folder      MacTCP Prep
                         PPP Preferences  
 
* - replace these, the other files will be created by the system

 MacPPP may have been configured for static IP address by putting an IP #
in the  IPCP Options box. That's a showstopper !
 But trashing the preferences file, re-installing, re-configuring and
restarting will take care of all that.

 If the IP address is truly 'server assigned', just check the 'Server'
button, enter the DNS info and get outa' there. Don't fill in any subnet
data or anything. You doh't even need to tell it the gateway address.
 MacTCP will get that info *for* the user !

 Less is better here.

Hope something helps :)
..WR
 

