In article <48o3s4$ktd@access4.digex.net>, rdd@access4.digex.net (R. D. Davis) writes:
> A few minutes go, I just discovered part of the problem with my
> failing DEC 871C power controller, which now makes me somewhat wary of
> some DEC power controllers.  What I had examined carefully was the
> circuitry inside of the power controller, and didn't see anything
> melted or charred looking.  What I failed to examine was the plug at
> the end of the power cord!  The formerly plastic around one of the
> plug connectors was black, and bending that connector slightly causes
> the charred plastic to crumble.

  This has been noted by DEC in the past (in particular, DEC claimed that
the Arrow-Hart [that's a brand] plug had "insufficent clamping force",
which led to a rather heated (no pun intended 8-) argument between A-H and
DEC).

  The actual fault in this case was DEC's. These plugs are designed to
clamp onto specific gauges of wire. DEC tends to use smaller wires than
the plug should be used for (in particular, I've seen lots of #16 with
20A plugs [as an example, the 11X44 configuration]). In cases like these,
you need to make sure that the screws are *really* tight, and there's no
substitute for using the proper wire.

  The failure you have is unlikely to be related to excessive current
draw, but more to a voltage drop causing heat. Just as space heaters can 
glow red-hot without tripping a 15A breaker, you can overheat a connec-
tor without tripping.

