In article <48oakp$89j@caesar.ultra.net>,
Carl R. Friend <carl.friend@swec.com> wrote:

>   Was the affected power connector here equipped with screw terminals for 
>the 
>main power cable (or some similar arrangemnent)? If so, and I admit some
>embarrassment at not thinking of it, those connections must be tight. Very
>tight.

This plug has the type of connectors where one inserts the stripped
wire into the plastic around the screw/clamp terminal and tightens the
screw.  It's not necessary to wrap the wires around the screw.
Unfortunlately, I can't tell how tightly the connection on this
terminal was, as the wire itself seems to have burned to bits, melted,
etc.

Note: the power cable used appears to be made from 14 gauge wire,
which, according to a chart that I have, means that it's limit is 15A
(for stranded copper wire) So, perhaps the system must have been
drawing between 15A and 16A.  Of course, I'd think that the entire
cable would have been rather warm, not just this end of the cable.
So, perhaps the cable wasn't connected properly at the plug.  Of
course, that's still no excuse for DEC not using the proper gauge of
wire.  I'm going to replace it with 12-guage wire, and send the bill
for it, and the new socket, to DEC... better yet... I wonder if DEC
would send a field servoid out here to repair it, and examine the
sockets in the power controller that this plug was plugged into.

What a melted and charred mess the inside of this plug is!  Firstly, I
noticed that the plastic, that one inserts the wire into before
tightening the screw for the "hot" connector, had complete melted and
disintegrated.  It no longer exists, except for some ashes.  All
that's there is the burned/rusty remains of the metal connector
itself, which didn't melt.  The wire itself that connected to that
connector broke off when I opened the plug, so it must have been just
barely connected., hence the dimming light on the power controller.
The insulation on this wire melted all the way up to where the power
cable's outer insulation was stripped (and some of that insultion is
slightly browned for about a half inch).

The blades of the plug all look normal.  However, the plastic around
the other two screw-terminals was also somewhat melted and cracked,
and the other screws are also rusty looking, like metal that's been in
a fire.  It must have been pretty darned hot inside that plug, and for
all I know, it my have been on fire.  This plug and short section of
cable are about 1 foot away from me, and the smell is still bad enough
to irritate my nose.  

Fortunately, this occured inside a garage with concrete walls and a
concrete ceiling!  I'd have been rather angry if it had set off a fire
and destroyed my 11/73 and 11/44 systems, and the 1967 VW Beetle that
are in there.

>   This may not have been a case of overloading the power controller at all;
>it might have been simply one of loose connectors. Screw tightness in power
>connectors is not something that gets checked frequently. 

Yes, this is easily overlooked... not a good thing to overlook.

>   I remember having unexplainable troubles with a PDP-10 a (long) while ago,
>and the problem was that the wires in the 3-phase mains connector had loosened
>up and were arcing. The plug itself was virtually destroyed and we were lucky
>we didn't have a fire on our hands! The only thing connected to that plug was
>the -10 itself; the line certainly was not overloaded.

A close call. 

>   If this can happen in a temperature-controller computer room (where
>component
>creep shouldn't be a problem) the possibilities must be greater in a home.

Worse yet in a semi-heated garage where the temperatures in some parts
of it go down in the fifties, and possibly the upper 40's when the
outside temperature go way below freezing on the coldest days.

>   Check your connections, everybody! I'm checking mine once I finish this
>post. Nobody needs a fire.

Thanks for the warning.  I hope that everyone else heeds it!  I'm
going to go check the other power controller, and the big 10 guage
extention cord and 30A twist lock connector, etc. that take power into
the garage to the 741D power controller.

Also, please check the gauge of the wiring used, and replace it if
it's not right.

>   By the way, Mr Davis.... Investigate the condition of the socket that the
>machine was plugged into. 

Thanks; I plan to.  

