We were able to play with a Pentium Pro P6 @ 166Mhz running Windows NT
build 1057 with Pro Engineer running a Pro-e Trail file:

P6 @ 166	vs.	21164 @ 366Mhz
128MB RAM		128MB RAM
2 Gig SCSI-2		4 Gig SCSI-3
8:22 (min/sec.)	time	46 seconds

Mixed integer and floating point.  The 21164 is a "hot rod" part
used in our lab (we have some 400Mhz running too).  Factoring clock
speed integer and floating point mix, the P6@166Mhz is about the 
speed 266Mhz 21064a.  However, a 200Mhz P6 part will be approx.
equal to a 266Mhz 21164.  

By the time Intel gets the 200Mhz part in production, Alpha
systems with 21164a will arrive with clock speeds of 400+Mhz
will be on the seen.  (Of course, there's a 21264 on the 
horizon!)

This jump by Intel is its closiest to Digital's offering to date, and
is quite impressive.  However, looking into both Intel's and Digital's
"crystal ball" (as well as the psychic friends network . . .:-) ) -
Digital will not fall behind, at least not in this century!

Windows NT and Digital Semiconductor Alpha AXP. . .long may they run!!

