In a related vein...

Many years ago (1965), I had a summer job at Beckman Instrument's
precision resistor factory.   The "low-precision" ones were
only 0.1%, and 0.01% was "run-of-the-mill".   My job was final
resistance testing, using a galvanometer connected to a large,
heavy resistance bridge.   It was set using tapered brass plugs
which were inserted into tapered holes between the resistance
elements.

Every morning, I had to clean and polish the brass plugs
and holes.   This was required in order to keep stray resistance
to an absolute minimum.   The final step was to coat the
surfaces with a very thin layer of ordinary Vasolene petroleum
jelly and wipe it off.

I have often wondered if this step actually did any good,
or if there are better substances to use.   I have not tried
it on any of my DEC boards, relying instead on Texwipe's
"Gold Wipes".   Of course, this may also be just so much voodoo.   :-)

