In article <48rp47$g5r@access1.digex.net>,
R. D. Davis <rdd@access1.digex.net> wrote:
>
>I'm back.  The resistance with J1 (the name I used for the connector
>that plugs into the circuit board) plugged in is about 880 ohms.

That's probably about right for the DC resistance of a small
transformer.

>>>  I'm
>>>trying to figure out why the pilot lamp dims when a light load is
>>>plugged in and why the power controller switches, or tries to switch,
>>>itself off when nothing shorted, or nothing at all is plugged into it.
>>
>>When you say "switch itself off", do you mean that the breaker is
>>tripping?
>
>Yes, that's what I meant.

Then you've either got a short or an over-sensitive breaker.

>>Yes, but you measured the resistance at a few volts.  Ohm's law is
>>really not an absolute law, but something like the "serving suggestion"
>>picture you see on all the food packaging.  Nobody in real life actually
>>prepares the food exactly that way; similarly, no material in real
>>life obeys ohm's law under all conditions.  In particular, if one of the
>>MOV's has begun to go bad, it may begin to conduct appreciably 
>>when there is 100 to 150 volts across it.  And it may be the case that
>>the inductive spike caused by turning something on or off is enough to
>>cause the MOV to begin to conduct and latch up, causing the
>>symptoms you are mentioning.
>
>I overlooked that.  Any suggestions for testing an MOV, aside from
>rigging up an MOV testing device, or cutting into the circuit and
>testing the current through the MOV when the circuit is powered up?

Uh, remove the MOV and see if the breaker still trips?  If removing
the MOV improves things, then the MOV was probably the problem.

Not only are MOV's nonlinear (in the respect of not obeying ohm's
law), but they also exhibit some hysteresis - after you go over
the voltage where they begin to conduct, you can go back down in
voltage below the original trip point and they will still conduct.
I think it may be more of a time rather than a voltage effect, as
after they lock up on 120 VAC they tend to stay locked up.

>(which could be a handy thing to have; it would be quite easy to
>design - a power cord, a relatively tame inductive load of some sort,
>such as a bouncy switch connected to a lightbulb, that throws a small
>spike into the circuit, two spring-clip jacks and a fuse).  This could
>be quite handy for testing new or surplus MOVs before using them in a
>circuit.

*Never* use surplus (unknown condition) MOVs.  It's just too much of
a hassle when they don't work properly - either by tripping at too
high or too low a voltage.  It's just so easy to get new MOVs.

And, to some extent, testing a MOV is somewhat destructive to it.
MOV's are rated for some total power absorption over their lifetime,
and after you've used this up they're likely to begin to go flakey.
So it's somewhat a destructive test.  (Like when Calvin asked his
Dad how they know what weight limits to place on bridges, and his
Dad told him that they build the bridge, drive bigger and bigger trucks
over it until it collapses, and then rebuild it and put up a sign
containing the weight of the heaviest truck that drove over it.)

>  One could also add a big coil and another switch, to create
>nice spikes for a more strenuous test.  

Oh, we had a nice spike generator here in my lab a year or so ago.  The
electrical wiring is quite old, and dates from when W.K.Kellogg (yes,
the cereal king) donated the money for construction in 1939.  Up
here on the third floor, we have three DEC Alpha workstation and a HP
workstation on the same 20-amp fuse.  When a janitor decided
to plug a big heavy-duty floor waxer into that circuit, the floor
waxer (basically a rather large electric motor) began turning for a few
seconds before the fuse blew.  Now remember your basic electricity?
What happens when you suddenly open a very inductive circuit (like
the floor waxer motor) when it has a lot of current going through it?
Big voltage spikes.  Where are those spikes going to go?  Into the
computers.  Actually, the HP's power supply served as a big surge
suppresser in this case - by taking the hit, and thus saving the Alphas.

>All sorts of fun possibilities
>come to mind.  A nice high-voltage stress-test circuit would be quite
>nice; something that looks like something out of Grandpa Munster's
>lab/dungeon would be rather nice indeed, something that hums nice and
>loud, causes lights to dim, produces some nice controlled arcing and
>perhaps even a few sparks.  I'd like that!

Oh, you ought to see some of the stuff we've got around here.  There's
a ion source we have that has been known to kill disk drives from
30 feet away.  And room 100 (the lab I get to play in) does look
a lot like Grandpa's Munster's lab...

>I'm anxious to see what happens after I replace the 14-guage line cord
>with a 12-gauge cord, and the hook up the new plug to it.  

I don't think this will make much difference - but it probably won't
hurt any.  I'm pretty sure your problem is *after* the circuit
breaker, not before it.

>By the way,
>it's only got one MOV.  Not very good protection, I suppose.

I *think* most of my power controllers have at least two MOV's, maybe
three.  I only tear them apart when they break, or begin buzzing, which
(as I think Tony has already pointed out) is a sign that they will
soon break unless you replace the filter capacitor for the relay.

Tim. (shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu)
Kellogg Radiation Lab, Caltech.



