Subject: WARNING: DEC 871C and 841D Power Controllers & Overloading
Date: 1995/11/19
Message-ID: <48o3s4$ktd@access4.digex.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 119655419
organization: Express Access Online Communications, Greenbelt, MD USA
newsgroups: vmsnet.pdp-11,alt.sys.pdp11,comp.sys.dec

A few minutes go, I just discovered part of the problem with my
failing DEC 871C power controller, which now makes me somewhat wary of
some DEC power controllers.  What I had examined carefully was the
circuitry inside of the power controller, and didn't see anything
melted or charred looking.  What I failed to examine was the plug at
the end of the power cord!  The formerly plastic around one of the
plug connectors was black, and bending that connector slightly causes
the charred plastic to crumble.

Now then, here's what I find to be somewhat alarming.  This is a
standard 20A plug:

         | -
          o 

This standard 20A plug sort of melted and it didn't trip the 16A
circuit breaker of the 871C power controller that it was plugged into.

Furthermore, the 874D power controller has outlets which are all
(according to the front label, but I suspect that they are actually
20A, not 16A, outlets) rated at 16A, which apparently aren't protected
against currents higher than 16A flowing through them, yet are less
than the main breaker's trip rating, which is a bit higher.

Although the 16A breaker in the 871C eventually tripped, it tripped
after it had apparently damaged itself (it's difficult to switch on
and now trips with nothing plugged into any of the 871C's sockets),
and, more frightening, it allowed the plug to melt and smolder, which
could have caused a fire, before it finally decided to trip
immediately after I'd turned the system on the last time, but not
while it was smoldering before that!!!

Other things that are somewhat unsettling: the 871D controller that
this 20A plug was plugged into was plugged into a socket that was
plugged into a circuit on a branch panel with a 20A circuit breaker.
This 20A breaker didn't trip either when the 20 plug melted.  So, either
the plug used on the DEC power controller was defective, or, circuit
breakers aren't as safe as I thought, or I'm overlooking something.

I've heard that circuit breakers aren't as reliable as fuses, and this
appears to be, from what I can tell, possible proof.

Has anyone else experienced a similar problem resulting from using a
power controller that may have been slightly overloaded?  When I got
this system, it had been working fine for years using the same power
controller (no, the plug wasn't burnt when I got it).  Apparently, my
addition of an RX02 floppy unit was the "straw that broke the camel's
back". so to speak.  However, I would have thought that if this was
too much of a load, it would have caused the circuit breaker to trip
much more quickly.  

Would anyone from DEC care to comment?  

One other thing: apparently, the acrid smell that I thought was coming
from the 11/73's rack, but couldn't figure out where, was actually
coming from where that system's power controller was plugged in.  Now,
I'm going to have to disassemble the 874D power controller and inspect
it for signs of damage. :-(

