rdd@access4.digex.net (R. D. Davis) wrote:

>A few minutes ago, I just discovered part of the problem with my
>failing DEC 871C power controller, which now makes me somewhat wary of
>some DEC power controllers. The formerly [white?] plastic around one of the
>plug connectors was black, and bending that connector slightly causes
>the charred plastic to crumble.

   The above has been edited somewhat to preserve bandwidth.

   Was the affected power connector here equipped with screw terminals for the 
main power cable (or some similar arrangemnent)? If so, and I admit some
embarrassment at not thinking of it, those connections must be tight. Very
tight.

   This may not have been a case of overloading the power controller at all;
it might have been simply one of loose connectors. Screw tightness in power
connectors is not something that gets checked frequently. 

   I remember having unexplainable troubles with a PDP-10 a (long) while ago,
and the problem was that the wires in the 3-phase mains connector had loosened
up and were arcing. The plug itself was virtually destroyed and we were lucky
we didn't have a fire on our hands! The only thing connected to that plug was
the -10 itself; the line certainly was not overloaded.

   If this can happen in a temperature-controller computer room (where
component
creep shouldn't be a problem) the possibilities must be greater in a home.

   Check your connections, everybody! I'm checking mine once I finish this
post. Nobody needs a fire.

   By the way, Mr Davis.... Investigate the condition of the socket that the
machine was plugged into. 
 
							Regards,

						     Carl R. Friend
						<carl.friend@swec.com>




