In article <48o3s4$ktd@access4.digex.net>, rdd@access4.digex.net (R. D. Davis) writes:
> Now then, here's what I find to be somewhat alarming.  This is a
> standard 20A plug:
> 
>          | -
>           o 
> 
> This standard 20A plug sort of melted and it didn't trip the 16A
> circuit breaker of the 871C power controller that it was plugged into.
> 
> Furthermore, the 874D power controller has outlets which are all
> (according to the front label, but I suspect that they are actually
> 20A, not 16A, outlets) rated at 16A, which apparently aren't protected
> against currents higher than 16A flowing through them, yet are less
> than the main breaker's trip rating, which is a bit higher.

The fact that your 20A plug "sort of melted" does not indicate that
more than 20A was flowing through it.  It may mean that you simply had
a bad connection in it, leading to excessive IR losses, and hence
excess heat in the plug.  

If you had really overheated the plug by drawing more than its rated 
power through it there'd be no reason for just one of its prongs to be 
loose. 

