senderow@diva.EECS.Berkeley.EDU (Dan Senderowicz) wrote:
>The disk (190 MB) of my microvax (ba23) went dead, so I'm
>thinking on buying a new 1GB (or 2GB) that I've seen advertised
>ranging between 200 and 500 bucs (my controller is a RQDX3). Can
>I buy any IDE disk (seagate, quantum, etc.) besides DEC? If so,
>I would really appreciate any hints on the recommended settings
>(disktab, partitions, etc.) as well as the procedure. Thanks a
>lot.

I assume that your 190MB disk drive is a DEC RD54 or a Maxtor 2190. 
(Actually, after formatting, this is about 160MB).

This is an MFM drive.  As far as I know, the RD54/2190 is the largest
MFM drive you can get.  (The RQDX3 is an MFM controller.)

You can get a SCSI drive for your machine, but you also need a SCSI 
controller.  I would recommend one of the CMD units.  You can use 
standard SCSI drives with these controllers.  I run one with a 400MB 
SCSI disk.  I don't know what the used price for these controllers is, 
but I suspect that it will 
cost more than the drive.

As far as I know, there is no way to use an IDE drive in a MicroVAX II.

