Looking at the newfs man page for Digital Unix 3.2A says:

  -b block-size
            The block size of the file system in bytes.  Note that the block
            size is fixed at 8KB.

Which is pretty silly.  You might try using a smaller frag-size (the
-f option) and seeing if that helps you.  You might also want to raise
the number of inodes per byte from the default of 1 per 4096 bytes.
So try making a small test filesystem with:

	newfs -i 1024 -f 1024 /dev/rrz?? /dev/rrz?c

and seeing how the file space usage turns out.

