In article <DHyq6J.97z@world.std.com>,
Michael Moroney <moroney@world.std.com> wrote:
>I have an old Microvax II with an RQDX3 and an RD54 disk.  Either the RD54
>or the RQDX has died (disk makes squealing noises and buzzes, I remember
>something about a format, but I have another idea) The RD54 is slow, small
>compared to modern SCSI disks.  Rather than figuring out what is wrong 
>and fixing it, it would be nice to have SCSI disks.  The Microvax II was 
>produced before DEC discovered SCSI, however.  When the Microvax II was the 
>state of the art I remember "clone" makers having boards that made SCSI 
>disks look like RD5x disks.  Could anyone supply me with details?  Did 
>they work well enough to allow a Microvax II to boot from a SCSI disk?
>
>-Mike

There are still companies making new SCSI Q-bus controllers.  In
particular, I have a CMD CQD440-T/M that controls both tape *and* disk
SCSI devices, and I'm very happy with it.  Andromeda also makes SCSI
Q-bus controllers, and I know some who are very happy with them too.
The CMD controllers list at over $1000, though.  If you have a tighter
budget, you may want to find an older Sigma/Webster, Dilog, or Emulex controller
on the used market, for a couple of hundred dollars.  Make sure
it works with your software, though; with each release of VMS more
and more older third-party MSCP controllers have quit working as the
DEC folks who write device drivers have figured out how to "break" them.
(Luckily, this doesn't seem to be happening as often as it was 5 or
10 years ago.)

DEC also sells at least two SCSI controllers for the Q-bus.  One of them
is the KZQSA, but I'm not sure if this particular one will drive disks
in a Microvax II - DEC's catalog lists it as only for CD-ROMs and
tape drives.  List price on this controller is somewhere around $1500-$2000.
The other DEC Q-bus controller, the RQZX1, is intended for use on
PDP-11's and, according to the rumors I hear, cannot be configured
in a Microvax II as it has only the PDP-11 firmware for configuration.
After configuration, you may be able to move it to a Microvax II and use
it.

