In article <488ept$hlv@solaris.cc.vt.edu>, Matthew Kaufman <kaufman> writes:
|>I have a code which was written in C/C++, but utilizes the LAPACK linear
|>algebra libraries which were written in Fortran.  The code was developed on an
|>IRIX system, but I need to get the code up and running on a Dec Alpha.  So, I
|>need to know what libraries to include to handle the Fotran <---> C/C++
|>communication.  
|>
|>Since I don't have the Alpha, I don't have the manuals :(.  Anybody familiar
|>with the process?

If it's just C and Fortran, use the f77 driver to compile and load all the
sources - it knows what to do.  But with C++, you'd have to compile either
the C++ code or the Fortran code separately and then link them specifying
the appropriate libraries.  You can find what libraries are used by compiling
a test program and specifying -v - you'll get the driver commands listed on
the terminal, including the libraries specified to ld.

The library switches f77 uses are:

  -lUfor -lfor -lFutil -lm -lots -lc
