DEC products are fine, I use an alpha2100 with osf1 3.2b, and it flys. 

DEC pricing, documentation, and its licence system is the problem. It seems that
its the same old DEC/IBM ploy of milking every last drop out of customers.

As an academic site we got around 50% discount, but still hard copy doco
would have cost us several thousand dollars, and as for C++

DEC C++  - Unlimited user License       $6,950(remember its a 50% discount)

excluding sales tax
(1A$=0.75c)

Compare this with SGI, they provided us with the development option, which included
C++ and case tools, for 13 indys(each machine was allowed unlimited users) for a bit
over $3000(and IRIX 5.3 was free).

The licence system seems designed to stop access to any but those who are both legal,
and persistent(and thats if you can get them to give you the correct license in the
first place).

In Australia a support question seems to bounce back and forth b/w the seller, and DEC.
(when it gets sorted out the information is spot on)

Don't get me wrong,  I LIKE the alpha, you type "./configure"  and "make", and things 
work(unlike on the indys). The system is stable, and good to work with( you should 
find some thing other than tar/dump for backups, SGI bundle bru, it does LZH
compression, and is very good, it is available for osf1).

DEC programmers, and engineers, keep up the good work you have a great system,
pity about the rest.
