Subject: Re: keypdad in MAIL becomes undefined--help
Date: 1995/11/17
Message-ID: <1995Nov17.012038.1@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 119573780
sender: news@unixhub.SLAC.Stanford.EDU
references: <48hcjh$di0@rzsun02.rrz.uni-hamburg.de>
organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec

In article <48hcjh$di0@rzsun02.rrz.uni-hamburg.de>, 
    			Phillip Helbig <phelbig@hs.uni-hamburg.de> writes:
> If I am running MAIL, do BREAK and transfer to a UNIX box (Falco
> VT 220 clone through a DEC terminal server), then the mail keypad
> definitions are lost when I come back to MAIL on the VAX and I have
> the numeric keypad.  How can this behaviour be prevented?  I mean 
> automatically, not through the setup or whatever.

        What's happening  (apparently)  is  that  the  unix  machine  is
    reseting  the terminal to numeric keypad mode (or is it the terminal
    server, can terminal servers do that?).  In any case, when you enter
    MAIL, or any of a number of other  VMS  applications  that  use  the
    keypad,  behind  your  back, the terminal is forced into application
    keypad mode.  It appears that once you use BREAK to get out of MAIL,
    something or  someone  is  putting  the  terminal  back into numeric
    keypad  mode.   When you return to your MAIL application, it has  no
    way of checking and restoring application keypad mode.

        One way to prevent this is to SPAWN out of MAIL before you BREAK
    to the unix session.   This  works  for  TPU (EVE), and I'm assuming
    similar  mechanisms  are  used  for  MAIL.   In  TPU,  the  terminal
    settings...like  keypad  mode...are  saved  when   you   enter   the
    application,  and  restored when you exit.  Also during a spawn, the
    terminal settings are restored just prior to the spawn, and put back
    the way TPU (or MAIL) needs them when you return from the spawn.

        If spawning doesn't work, I  can  think of no _automatic_ way to
    restore  the  settings.   Your only other alternative  would  be  to
    prevent whoever is putting the terminal in numeric keypad mode  from
    doing that...

        -Ken
