In article <487m4v$sdf@topaz.sensor.com>, ron@topaz.sensor.com (Ron
Natalie) wrote:

> Well it may not be the intent of the engineers (such decisions rarely are),
> but DEC consinstantly offers the ARC versions of these boxes for less than
> identically configured SRM boxes.
> 
> -Ron

_both_ kinds of consoles are present in ALL Alphas that can run Windows
NT.  The one that is the "default" depends on your OS.  If you wade
through the startup menus on the NT console (ARC), you will find an option
that says "Switch to VMS/OSF console" or something really close.  If you
execute it, and then power-cycle the machine, you'll come up in the SRM
console.  If you had a disk with VMS or Digital Unix (nee OSF/1) on it,
you could boot it and run.

Conversely, from the SRM >>> prompt, if you did a SET OSTYPE NT, you would
get flipped into the ARC firmware after the next power cycle.  So if you
had a really beefed-up DEC AlphaServer 2100 5/300 running VMS or Digital
UNIX with all kinds of peripherals, you could issue one command and flip
it over to boot Windows NT -- if you wanted to and had a licensed copy
sitting around.

It's not the _console_ code that costs the big bucks. Rather, it's the OS
license.  And part of that reflects economies of scale, or lack thereof.
It's much easier to spread, say, a $100 million OS development cycle cost
over one million or two million copies (i.e., Windows NT) than over
100,000 or so (either of VMS or Digital Unix).

john

