In article <47tuf6$r69@tribune.mayo.edu>, hanson@rcf.mayo.edu (Russ Hanson) writes...
>I am running DECGKS software and use it to print to an ln03p laser printer.
>One of my users got an ln03 laser and I do not seem to be able to print to
>it with the ln03p driver. What is the difference in the 3 and the 3p.  Is
>the 3 upgradeable to the 3p? 

Hmmm, by LN03P could this be an LN03 PLUS printer? The easiest way to tell is
to print a test page--hit the "T" button on the back--and look in the upper
left corner for a "PLUS" firmware number. If your user has a base LN03 laser
printer (no "PLUS", same test) then the major difference is memory. The
original LN03 had relatively little memory (scandalously little compared to
today's printers) and couldn't reliably image a full page of graphics. The
"PLUS" version had enough memory built in to handle a full page bitmap. We used
to sell RAM cartridges (LN03X-CR) that plugged into the two holes in front of
the printer. When both were populated, that might help but I won't swear to it
since I haven't done any testing.
