In article <DHwEwI.A0x@mystica.uucp>, R.D. Davis <rdd@mystica.uucp> wrote:

>Nothing appears burned or melted, but the circuit breaker isn't
>exactly easy to switch into the closed position.

Is it easier to switch into closed when *not* plugged into the
wall?  If so, this indicates that something, somewhere, is shorting
out and causing the breaker to trip.

>  I've measured the DC
>input resistance; can anyone tell me if it appears correct?

Sorry; I've searched your posting high and low for this number,
but can't find it!  In any event, I'll try to offer some advice...

>  I'm
>trying to figure out why the pilot lamp dims when a light load is
>plugged in and why the power controller switches, or tries to switch,
>itself off when nothing shorted, or nothing at all is plugged into it.

When you say "switch itself off", do you mean that the breaker is
tripping?

>There don't appear to be any short circuits, and the input resistance
>is extremely high (about infinite) when J1 (the circuit board jack,
                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
does this mean "not quite infinity, but only about half that large?"
Sorry, I couldn't resist :-)

>not the panel jack) is unplugged, which seems, to me, to be normal.

Yes, but you measured the resistance at a few volts.  Ohm's law is
really not an absolute law, but something like the "serving suggestion"
picture you see on all the food packaging.  Nobody in real life actually
prepares the food exactly that way; similarly, no material in real
life obeys ohm's law under all conditions.  In particular, if one of the
MOV's has begun to go bad, it may begin to conduct appreciably 
when there is 100 to 150 volts across it.  And it may be the case that
the inductive spike caused by turning something on or off is enough to
cause the MOV to begin to conduct and latch up, causing the
symptoms you are mentioning.

>I'm probably going to try powering it up with J1 disconnected, and see
>if the circuit breaker still wants to trip.

Good test.  If you disconnect J1 and the breaker still wants to trip,
I'll bet just about anything that you've got a bad MOV or two in there.

Tim.



