Subject: Power controller questions
Date: 1995/11/11
Message-ID: <DHuuw3.83o@mystica.uucp>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 119077101
organization: The Computer Room in a Haunted Basement
newsgroups: vmsnet.pdp-11,alt.sys.pdp11,comp.sys.dec

Tonight, the 871C power controller in my 11/73 system began acting
rather strangely.

Firstly, I don't have the cabling to control the power controller from
the 11/73 chasis hooked up, so, to power up the system, I use the
following sequence: switch power controller on (this powers up the
BA23 expansion box and the RX02 floppy drive unit), power up each of
the Fujitsu M2311/2312 hard drives, the TS05 tape drive and finally
the BA23 containing the CPU.

Tonight, after turning the power-controller circuit breaker(s) on and
powering up one of the hard drives, I heard a "click kerchunk" sound
and saw a flash of light (apparently what I saw was the power
controller light blinking).  Quickly, I shut everything off and then
tried switching the power controller back on; the pilot light
flickered and dimmed.  I shut it off again.  Next, I unplugged
everything, trying each plug one at a time, to see if anything was
causing a short circuit.  Everything went fine until I plugged the
BA23 expansion unit in, but that was the only thing so far that wasn't
switchable and was drawing any current.  The light on the power
controller dimmed slightly when I turned the power controller on with
this unit plugged into it.

Then, again, with everything unplugged, the power controller light
flickered a bit when switched on, and it sounded for second or so like
the contactor didn't want to keep the contacts closed.

Is this the way that these power controllers typically fail?  Also,
due to the wiring situation here, I've got this smaller controller
plugged onto a larger controller, an 874C, in my 11/44's rack...
the reason being that the only electrical socket available in the
garage is the twist-on socket that the 11/44's controller plugs into.
Is there normally any problem with pluggine one power controller into
another one (I've been using it this way for over a year).

One other thing - recently, I've noticed a sort of acrid-like smell
coming from the system at times.  I've tried sniffing around to see
where it was coming from, but the sniff tests proved inconclusive,
and everything was working fine.  Does the power controller usually
produce an acrid smell before it begins behaving as noted above?

Apparently, it's time for me to remove and disassemble the power
controller to see if there's something melted in there. :-(

Until I fix this (hopefully it's not a difficult-to-find part causing
the problem - I'm suspecting the circuit breakers), I've possibly got
some working spares that I can use. However, the spres are non-DEC, so
I'm anxious to repair my genuine DEC power controller. :-)

By the way, is anyone familiar with some largish (and quite heavy)
power controllers in a large silver box, 8 outlets (6 switched, 2
unswitched), two 20A circuit breakers (tied together), two fan-output
connectors and one remote connector?  I don't think that the original
manufacturer's name is on these... I suspect that, like Applied Color
Systems stuck their name over DEC's on the DECwriter, that they've
just put their name on these controllers.  The model number on these
controllers is: C1035.

Thanks very much in advance for any information that anyone can
provide about any of the above.

Well... off to deinstall, take apart and inspect the 871C... :-)

