In article <DHuuw3.83o@mystica.uucp>, 
rdd@mystica.uucp (R.D. Davis) writes:

> Tonight, the 871C power controller in my 11/73 system began acting
> rather strangely.

My 861-F power controller failed in a similar fashion, so
you might want to consider this:

On the PC board, there is a capacitor that filters
the AC to the reed relay.   When that capacitor starts
to fail, the reed relay is not held in firmly.   Depending
on how rapidly it fails, the reed relay may vibrate,
causing the big contactor to not hold in firmly.

The inductive spikes from all this will eventually
zorch the MOVs and, in my case, zap the reed relay.
Since the reed relay in the 861-F is a special center-tapped
one, the only replacement was from DEC.

If it has not progressed to this point yet, try
replacing the small capacitor(s) on the PC board.

