This afternoon, I decided to trace out the schematics for my 871C
power controller (they're attached to this message), and ended up with
a few questions.

Nothing appears burned or melted, but the circuit breaker isn't
exactly easy to switch into the closed position.  I've measured the DC
input resistance; can anyone tell me if it appears correct?  I'm
trying to figure out why the pilot lamp dims when a light load is
plugged in and why the power controller switches, or tries to switch,
itself off when nothing shorted, or nothing at all is plugged into it.
There don't appear to be any short circuits, and the input resistance
is extremely high (about infinite) when J1 (the circuit board jack,
not the panel jack) is unplugged, which seems, to me, to be normal.
I'm probably going to try powering it up with J1 disconnected, and see
if the circuit breaker still wants to trip.

Can anyone fill in some of the details that I've not been able to figure
out yet about the controller's circuit, such as what "IC1" is?  I'm
gussing that it's either a small relay or some sort of IC.  Other
things... does anyone have any specs for transistor Q1?  Also, any
ideas as to what the numbers on the diodes are?  All that I can make
out so far is that they're in the 1N.... series.

Thanks very much in advance for any information that anyone can
provide about this.  Hopefully the schematics are readable... I'm not
used to creating many ASCII schematics.

Other things... the non-DEC (Applied Color Systems) power controllers
that I have are quite different than this one from DEC.  The biggest
difference, aside from appearance, is that they are mostly
electro-mechanical... no active components.  The ciruitry consists of,
quite simply, two ganged circuit breakers, a relay (contactor), a
transformer, pilot light, jacks (control and fans) and outlets!  I
haven't yet had a chance to trace the schematic for it.  However, it
would seem to me that it poses a better chance, if something goes
wrong, of frying something in the 11/73 CPU box that it's connected
to.  How good is the isolation in the BA23 where it connects to the
jack that connects to the power controller?  Any chance of there being
an opto-isolator in there?


