> flickered and dimmed.  I shut it off again.  Next, I unplugged
> everything, trying each plug one at a time, to see if anything was
> causing a short circuit.  Everything went fine until I plugged the
> BA23 expansion unit in, but that was the only thing so far that wasn't
> switchable and was drawing any current.  The light on the power
> controller dimmed slightly when I turned the power controller on with
> this unit plugged into it.
> 
> Then, again, with everything unplugged, the power controller light
> flickered a bit when switched on, and it sounded for second or so like
> the contactor didn't want to keep the contacts closed.

OK... Let me explain just what a power controller contains :

The incoming mains is filtered (most of the units contain a nice LC
filter), spikes are removed using MOV's and then the AC line is connected
to the neon and the input of the circuit breaker. The output of the breaker
goes to the unswitched outlets and to the input of a large contactor-like
relay. The output of this feeds the switched outlets. The coil of the contactor
is often (apart from the 860?) rated at mains votlage and is controlled by
a small reed-relay on the circuit board that provides isolation between the
mains supply and the control electronics. The control electronics is very
simple in concept - it turns on the reed relay iff the power on signal is active
(tied to ground) and the power-off signal is not. This logic is typically done
using either a couple of transistors (860), a specially wound differential reed
relay (861) or a constant current supply and diodes (872). I've never heard of
chips being used in this circuit. The power for the reed relay comes from a
little unregulated transformer/rectifier/smoothing cap circuit in the power
controller that provides typically 12-24V.

The first thing to check is that the incoming mains is stable. It may sound
silly, but the fault could be outside the power controller. Also, measure the
mains voltage (take care) across the input (and the switched sockets if you
like) while repeating the power-up tests. Note that the neon lamps in DEC power
units are not reliable - I once spent a day tracing a suspected problem in a
dual 860 power controller/H742 PSU system which turned out to be a defective
neon...

Assuming the mains is stable, and that it doesn't dip when you power up the
system, then you have a control logic problem. The most likely cause is a
defective reed relay - certainly in the UK, the relays are not suitable for
switching our 240V mains and suffer from burnt contacts. Replacing the reed
switch in the middle is probably easier than finding the correct spare relay,
unless your junk box is as wierd as mine...
Also check the smoothing cap on the controller PSU. This can loose capacitance
and cause relay chattering. Not common, but it has occured.

The rest of the logic is trivial to sort out. Measure the voltage drop  across
the relay contacts (and contactor contacts) when the unit is operating - if
there's any significant drop you have a burnt contact that will lead to
overheating later.

>
> Is this the way that these power controllers typically fail?  Also,

The normal problem in the UK is welded contacts on the reed relays in the 861,
which causes them not to turn off. I've had it several times, and in my last box
of DEC spares that I was given were a few spare relays, so it's probably
happened elsewhere..

> due to the wiring situation here, I've got this smaller controller
> plugged onto a larger controller, an 874C, in my 11/44's rack...
> the reason being that the only electrical socket available in the
> garage is the twist-on socket that the 11/44's controller plugs into.
> Is there normally any problem with pluggine one power controller into
> another one (I've been using it this way for over a year).

That should be OK - no reason for it not to work provided you don't overload
the 874. Of course, a high resistance connection in the 874 could cause voltage
drops to the 871.

>
> One other thing - recently, I've noticed a sort of acrid-like smell
> coming from the system at times.  I've tried sniffing around to see

Ouch... That needs to be sorted out. The most likely place is the BA23
power harness (I'm told they can catch fire! and that DEC did an improved
version), but it could also be a high resistance connection in the mains side
Basically, something has gone high-resistance and is dissipating more power than
it should.

> where it was coming from, but the sniff tests proved inconclusive,
