I've tidied up and corrected the schematic in part. C4 is still a puzzle 
- it can't go where it is shown - my guess is that it's put in parallel 
with C2. Anyway, as to how it works.

T1, D4,D5 and C2 form a simple unregulated PSU (biphase rectifier). Q1 
is, in fact an 8V regulator which tries to maintain 8V across R2, and 
thus a consstant current through it. Since the current flowing into pin 2 
of this device is small, it forms a (poor) constant-current supply.

When pin 1 of the reed relay IC1 is grounded, via diodes D1 and D2 (to 
give a 1.2V drop) and then either via Sw1a (in local mode) or swi1b (in 
remote mode), J2-3, the external power on switch, and J2-1, then the 
current flows through IC1's coil from pin 6 and energises it. The 
contacts close and complete the circuit between J1-1 and J1-2. This 
energises the contactor, and turns on the switched outlets. 
When the power off line J2-2 is grounded, then the diode D3 is connected 
from the output of the current source to ground. This effectively shorts 
out IC1's coil, which thus turns off. In practice, the overheat switch is 
connected here to turn off the machine if it overheats.

  
