In article <47oc8j$qop@engr.orst.edu>,
Jason Moore <moorej@research.CS.ORST.EDU> wrote:
>I was wondering if any of you can tell me more about the structure of
>Alpha page tables.  I know (from CACM, Feb 93) that the first level
>points to 2 2nd level entries - one shared and one private.

Get copies of the chip and architecture reference manuals.  I don't
recognise the info you quote, but the structure is largely PALcode
defined.

There are three levels of page table in the PAL implementations I know
of (I don't know what NT does, but then it's only a Micro$oft
`operating system', isn't it? -- there are disturbing "NT mode"
mumbles in the 21164 manual which I haven't completely understood).

Each level contains 2**10 pages more than the one above.  Thus the top
level has one page in it (8192 bytes) which represents 1024 second
level page table pages, and each second level page represents 1024
third-level page table pages.

>My question is about the 2nd and 3rd levels.  How many entries in each?

1024 and 1048576

>Is any special technique used to cache these, or are they subject to
>removal from cache like any other data?

There are I-space and D-space TLBs.  Page table pages get treated as
data, of course, so that first- and second-level pages only ever
appear as D-space, but the third-level could be either.

As data, they're not treated in any special way by the cache.
--
