In article <47oc8j$qop@engr.orst.edu>, moorej@research.CS.ORST.EDU (Jason Moore)
writes:

>I was wondering if any of you can tell me more about the structure of
>Alpha page tables.  I know (from CACM, Feb 93) that the first level
>points to 2 2nd level entries - one shared and one private.
>
>My question is about the 2nd and 3rd levels.  How many entries in each?
>Is any special technique used to cache these, or are they subject to
>removal from cache like any other data?
>

The answers to many of these questions vary depending on which operating system
you're talking about, particularly the questions about how page tables might
move in and out of cache.

How many entries are in each varies with the size of one's virtual address
space.  If the question you're asking is how many page table entries will fit
in a single page table page, I believe the answer is 1024 for OpenVMS and
Digital Unix, as both operating systems use a 64 bit page table entry, and
all extant implementations of the architecture employ an 8192 byte page.
For Windows NT it's 2048, because Windows NT employs 32-bit page table entries.

