In article <47p062$s1e@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>, gerhardt@agnt69.dechub.lkg.dec.com (Keith Gerhardt) writes:
> There are many examples of ethernet controllers that do not work with the 90C.
> All of these have crystal oscillators that are out of spec.  early Suns are 
> a good example.  no BS, just the facts.

  This may very well be. However, in the Netport the timing is done in the
Ethernet controller chip (82586) which then feeds the same signals at logic
levels to the circuitry that turns it into either an AUI port or a BNC port.
Putting a BNC transceiver on the AUI port makes the device work with the
90C.

  If you were seeing "clock creep", it would only affect the larger packet
sizes - shorter ones would pass through Ok. No packets from the Netport make
it through.

  Lastly, the industry is littered with dead products from companies that
took the position "we're standards compliant - everybody else is broken".
Even if it was these various non-DEC devices that were in the wrong (which
hasn't been proven by anything said here), I would think that since DEC is
the only vendor that's having a problem with it, that DEC would be inter-
ested in a workaround.

