In article <622166222wnr@natron.demon.co.uk>,
Roger Barnett  <Roger@natron.demon.co.uk> wrote:
>
>Digital's announcement of their I86 to Alpha binary translator 
>mentions that they have used the same technology in five other
>cases; well VAX/VMS and Sparc/Sunos are two, anyone know what the
>other three were ?

Hmmm... five other cases implies several that DEC hasn't mentioned thus far

By my count, we have:

VAX/VMS to Alpha/VMS (VEST)
MIPS/Ultrix to Alpha/DEC OSF/1 (mx)
SPARC/SunOs 1 to Alpha/DEC OSF/1 (FreePort Express)
FX!32

DEC demoed an FX!32 precursor more than two years ago. Problem was, it 
was not user-transparent as FX!32 is. That could account for one of the 
other cases.

And if you go way back in history, DEC in 1985 was working on a RISC 
architecture called SAFE (Simplified Architecture for Fast Execution). 
The hardware never saw the light of day, but the software savants 
(reportedly) developed a VAX Macro to SAFE binary instruction translator. 
That could be another case.

Finally, it's possible that VAX to PRISM code translation was developed 
as part of the PRISM RISC project. 

Considering that DEC thus far has used binary translation to enable 
interoperability between Alpha and four source architectures, it seems 
only logical that additional source platforms are being targeted as we speak.

