In article <1995Nov4.195215.214@mediatex.ch>,
Peter J. Neff * MEDIAtex AG <pn@mediatex.ch> wrote:
>I have two problems with Pathworks:
>
>When I copy a big (10-20 MB) file from the PC harddisk to a VMS file service,
>sometimes it times out in the creation phase of the file. And the file is
>not copied. It's just allocated on the VAX but nothing is copied.
>I think, there is sum kind of timeout which is set to low, but I don't know
>what value I have to change.
>
>Second problem, when I copy files form PC to the VAX or otherwise, I get
>only poor (50-100 kBps) throughput. I know, ethernet has a physical capacity
>of 1000 kBps minus overhead should give at least 500 kBps throughput.
>When I copy the files to a NT server (Intel 80486-33 Based!) I get 200 kBps
>without a problem!
>What can I do to increase the troughput?
>
>The technical data:
>
>Server:	VAXserver 4300
>		32 M Ram
>		2 GB free Disk Space (Total 6 GB)
>		VMS 5.5-2
>		DECNET Phase 4
>		Pathworks 5.0c (Protocol DECNET)
...
>
>Client:	PC with Pentium 100
>		DE435 (PCI-Based) Network Adapter
>		40 MB Ram
>		Windows 95
>		Pathworks for Windows 95

This sounds similar to a problem we're having with VMS Pathworks.
(DEC's working on it, but hasn't gotten back to us yet.)

Using VAX VMS 5.5-2, Windows for Workgroups 3.11, and VMS Pathworks
V5.0-302C (NETBeui), we have problems with large file copies from the
VAX to a PC *if* the file is a variable-length format file.  Sometimes
the file just takes way too to long to copy, and sometimes the copy
aborts completely--in Windows File Manager you get the misleading
error "Access is denied.  Make sure the disk is not full or
write-protected" (in face, the disk is neither).

Our workaround is to convert the variable-length file to stream
format.  We don't have the problem with stream files or fixed-length
files.

We use:

  convert infile outfile /fdl=tostream.fdl

where tostream.fdl contains:

  IDENT   "23-MAY-1995 09:28:29   VAX-11 FDL Editor"

  RECORD
          FORMAT                  stream


Hope this helps.

-- 
Marshall Abrams                              ab2r@midway.uchicago.edu
(The one person on the net with no home page--not in progress, not planned.)



