Subject: Re: SRM vs ARC console firmware
Date: 1995/11/08
Message-ID: <47osk6$rvg@itnews.sc.intel.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118862312
references: <1995Nov6.080529.652@flying-disk.com> <47m24j$d6n@hermes.oc.com>	<1995Nov7.092658.659@flying-disk.com> <x3lopsf5c5.fsf@avante.WPI.EDU>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Intel
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: vmsnet.alpha,comp.sys.dec
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1N (Windows; I; 32bit)


The ARC console was developed as part of the ACE Initiative sponsored by 
MIPS, DEC, SCO, Compaq and Microsoft in 1991. It was included in the ARC 
(Advanced RISC Computing) Specification. Microsoft played a key role in 
defining the ARC spec, which survived the ACE initiative. The ARC console 
uses the DOS FAT file system and generally takes a minimalist attitude in 
that the role of the console is to load an OS loader and then disappear.

The SRM console evolved from the VAX/VMS boot process and became part of 
the Alpha SRM (System Reference Manual). Since NT was not initially 
planned for Alpha, there was no notion of an ARC console. When NT was 
added to the Alpha plate, it was too late to switch to a single console. 
NT was on a path using ARC, and OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 were far along the 
SRM path.The AlphaStations and AlphaServers support both consoles so they 
can run all 3 OSes. Both consoles include x86 emulation for running BIOS 
code in PCI adapters.

There was a serious attempt to unify the console a while back, but the 
amount of work and schedule made it impractical.

A side effect of the two consoles is that it allows for product 
differentiation as evidenced by the ALpha XL for NT vs AlphaStations.

Dileep (who gave up on console unification in 1994)
