Message-ID: <1995Nov8.131403.2422@yrsk.demon.co.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118930906
x-nntp-posting-host: yrsk.demon.co.uk
references: <47oiqj$2ce@tally.tally.com>
organization: Yezerski Roper Ltd
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <47oiqj$2ce@tally.tally.com>, kerry@tally.com writes:
> We have a VAXStation 3100 that has gone belly-up on us today and having
>  been given the task of finding out why, discovered we have an incomplete
>  set of manuals.  In looking for the self test diagnostics, the Ultrix index says that 
> the explanation of these diagnostics is in Reference Vol.1, which we don't have.
> Could some kind soul point me to somewhere on the 'Net that would have the
> explanation of these diagnostics, or decode for me the following diagnostic
> pattern:
> 
> 7...6?..5...4_..3_..2_..1..
> 
Are you really running Ultrix on a VS3100 - most people bought Decstation
3100s for that ?  Still, the O/S is irrelevant as you have a hardware
problem.

If the number is followed by ..., it tested OK.
If the number is followed by _.., it either wasn't tested or was missing.
If the number is followed by ?.., it failed some test in a minor sense.
If the number is followed by ??., it failed some test in a major sense.

6 is the external SCSI controller.  At a guess, you have a model 30/38/40/48
in which case there is in fact only one SCSI board for both A and B chains.
The B chain dangles out on the back, whereas normally all internal devices
are on the A chain.

You don't appear to have a catastrophic problem.  It could be something as
simple as an unterminated SCSI chain.  Try TEST 50 at the >>> prompt and
look for the line containing SCSI-B and some hex digits, followed by another
line indicating the status of each device - make sure any external devices
are connected and powered up.

Was there not a further line of output like ? 6 00A0 0000.4001 ??  (This
particular one is no more than an absence of devices on the chain, without
a termination.)

If your system is automatically booting at power-up, you want to hit the
Halt button (small square one next to the Ethernet connectors) and type
SET HALT 3.  This will have the effect of remaining at console mode after
power-up self-tests.

-- 
