references: <4796hs$fv7@newton.uncg.edu>
organization: Nauticom - Internet Access Provider
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

The WS42A-DA is the basic kernal for the VS3100.  It is a KA42A-BA system 
board, with a monochrome adapter.  

The daughterboards are probably memory expansion boards.  Should be easy 
to figure out, just take them out and look at them.  They probably have 
the part number etched, but in any case memory boards all look the same - 
rows and rows of almost identical chips (oversimplified, but you get the 
picture).  One of the memory boards will be seated to a connector on the 
motherboard, and the 2nd memory  board will be seated to a connector on 
the first memory board.  If that is how your boards are mounted, then 
they are certainly memory boards.  They would both be mounted towars the 
front of the system.

If you have 2 boards, both mounted on the main system board, then the big 
square one is the graphics board, and smaller retangular one is the 
single memory upgrade board (ie, you *might* be able to expand the 
memory, see above).  The graphics board will probably have etched on it's 
top side (so you have to take it out to look at it), something like 
"8-plane graphics."

--reed


