In article <47e1qe$jm7@gazette.bcm.tmc.edu>, slutes@bcm.tmc.edu (Steve Lutes) says:
>
>I'm trying to read a PDP-11 binary file using Visual Basic in Excel 5.0
>on a Power PC 8500. I can read strings and integers fine, but long
>integers and floating point numbers go haywire. Can someone tell me
>what to do?

For Integers it's not too hard

On the PC long integers bytes are stored

3 2 1 0

If I remember correctly PDPs often stored as:

1 0 3 2

Note that this is not a big/little endian shift.  A
68000 would store the bytes

0 1 2 3

For floating point you have some hard work ahead of you.  The
PDP does not use a standard floating point format. You are
going to need to get a copy of the PDP architecture manual
(The bit layout is really screwy.) and move the bits around 
yourself.

John

