>I have a DECstation 3100 with no hardware manuals.  For a while now,
>when it boots, it counts down from 7 to 2 and then reports a failure.
>Nothing much else seems to go wrong, so I've been ignoring it.

 [ snip ]

>My question is twofold.  First, could somebody tell me what the
>failure at step 2 at boot time means?  I now assume that it refers to
>the Ethernet interface, but I would like to make sure.  Second, could
>somebody give me some advice on how to get this fixed?  I have a
>feeling that if I can Digital technical support it will cost me an arm
>and a leg.  This is just a home system, so there is a limit on how
>much I can spend.  It is worth some money to me to get it working,
>though, as I use the DECstation for software development (I wrote and
>distribute a free version of UUCP).

Selftest 2 _is_ an ethernet problem.  We've mostly seen it here when
there's faulty thin-net cabling around.  If you're using thin coax,
check and re-check your cables and termination.

I believe 3100's (and 2100's for that matter) sometimes have a problem
after a hard or soft reset that can cause problems too - try a power
cycle and see if that makes a difference.

Simon.
