Subject: Re: Using WABI on a Vaxstation 4000-90
Date: 1995/11/06
Message-ID: <RAY.BELLIS.95Nov6103519@axp01.mrc-bbc.ox.ac.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118640099
references: <bfogarty-0511951701540001@bfogarty.fast.net>
organization: O.U. Dept. of Experimental Psychology
newsgroups: bit.listserv.dectei-l,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.dec.micro,cu.decstation.managers,swnet.sys.dec,uiuc.sys.dec,umn.local-lists.decstation-man,vmsnet.decus.lugs

In article <bfogarty-0511951701540001@bfogarty.fast.net> bfogarty@fast.net (Ben Fogarty) writes:

> We have several users who are running VMS 5.5-2HW on DEC Vaxstation 4000's
> with Motif version 1.1. They are anxious to connect to a sun machine
> running WABI version 2.0 to permit the emulation of PC software. We find
> we can connect, but once WABI starts and creates the subdirectory in their
> local directory, it chokes on a font problem. We have contacted DEC and
> sun on the problem and both spent a great deal of effort in finger
> pointing. I believe more than one person out there has tried to use a 4000
> to run WABI on a sun machine to do PC type stuff. I also believe there is
> probably a set of fonts somewhere that can be downloaded to permit smooth
> operation of this combination of machines. Has anyone out there
> encountered/solved the problem? Would you please share with a system
> manager who has less VMS experiences and more unix knowledge? 

If the WABI setup is anything like SoftWindows as far as font handling
goes then your X *server* requires the correct fonts to be installed
in the fontpath on the machine running the server.  This may, of course,
infringe Sun's copyright on the WABI fonts.

If the VS4000 X server supports the X11R5 fontserver (fs) then you
should be able to get around this by running `fs' on the Sun and then
pointing your X server at the Sun using the VMS equivalent of

% xset +fp tcp/hostname:7000

This way the fonts stay on the Sun's disk and get called up across the
network as neccesary.

Ray.

