In article <474qr3$ha3@newsserv.zdv.uni-tuebingen.de>, zrasl01@alphamor.zdv.uni-tuebingen.de (Laurentios Servissoglou) writes:
|>Hello,
|>
|>Every time a user runs the C-Compiler on our DEC ALPHA 3300 with the flag
|>'-migrate -taso' the program gemc_cc is executed. This executable is in the
|>following directory:
|> 
|>   /usr/ccs/lib/cmplrs/cc
|>
|>Do you know what this program makes? It's the biggest one in this directory and
|>every time it runs the machine is full of swap activity (no one else can work).

gemc_cc is DEC C.  
With V3.* of the OS, invoking 
  cc -migrate
means "invoke the DEC C compiler, instead of the usual "acc" compiler.

Effective V4.0 (now in external field test), DEC C (gemc_cc) will become
the default compiler. This means you will no longer need to specify -migrate
to get it. (To get the acc compiler, you'll need to specify -oldc or something
like that).

Tom


