From: djp@zeus.unx.dec.com (Dave Parker)
Subject: Re: Different partition sizes?
Date: 1995/11/03
Message-ID: <47d332$2mv@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118559783
distribution: world
references: <46u5dcINN55f@oak.cis.ohio-state.edu>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: djp@zeus.unx.dec.com (Dave Parker)
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <46u5dcINN55f@oak.cis.ohio-state.edu>, block@cis.ohio-state.edu (brad block) writes:
|>
|>Hello
|>
|>Am I allowed to make partition sizes anything I want or are there
|>some numbers I need to conform to, for example, partition a = 64mb,
|>b = 128mb, c = entire disk, etc?
|>
|>- Thanks!
|>BRad
|>

You can pretty much do as you please.  The default partition sizes are designed
especially for using the disk to contain root on the 'a' partition, swap on the
'b' partition, and usr on the 'g' partition.  If you aren't using the disk in
this fashion (and even if you are) you don't have to use that paritioning
scheme.

Conventionally, both 'a' and 'c' partitions have an offset of 0.  You might
consider sticking to that convention.  Also, 'c' generally spans the entire
disk.

Dave



