In article <47e7t4$k0j@overload.lbl.gov> zfliu@leea.cchem.berkeley.edu (Zhifeng Liu) writes:
ZL>    The comp.sys.intel group has been buzzling with the excitement about
ZL> the soon-to-be released 200 MHz Pentium Pro systems.  Some netters are
ZL> even claiming that such a Pentium Pro system would be as fast as the
ZL> AlphaStation 5/266, while the price for a Pentium Pro machine is 
ZL> substantially lower.  

ZL>    Would anyone like to shed some lights on this subject, from the 
ZL> perspective of AlphaStation users?
--

I doubt you'll be seeing the Pentium Pro in workstations in the
near-term, at least not in commodity pricing.

To make effective use of this chip, you need to run something other
than Win95, such as W/NT or Unix. Running a 386-oriented binary on the
Pentium Pro makes the performance go down, so I've been told. So, most
vendors are initially targetting the server market.

As a side note, you can buy an Alpha XL-266 266Mhz running W/NT for
$6000, with 17" monitor and 32MB of memory. That's a more accurate
comparison, I would warrent.





